<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Considering they=E2=80=99d just gone nine =
games without a win, assorted Manchester United fans carping about the mann=
er of their team=E2=80=99s Bigger Cup win over Roma on shouty radio phone-i=
ns on Tuesday night struck Football Daily as the type who=E2=80=99d complai=
n about a firefighter refusing to wipe their boots at the front door before=
 rescuing them from a burning house. Yes, it=E2=80=99s true that United pla=
yed appallingly in the first half and were lucky to be only 2-0 down. And y=
es, the penalty was somewhat fortuitous against a knack-ravaged Serie A sid=
e <a href=3D"https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/u001.Yw_JkLMEmFu=
ifc_XG18IRyTNtZQ7fIEMgszcCSneHEBgtjhYSJYcaNsDra0BxnPgUZY4A7HrkHwEAUW5C7uPar=
6V7CtXbFUT_Oylo_VAICsNTNPw0UDAsKQSoysg7dWY0DlrBQpuux32o51z9tSgGlEaBUWe1AIjE=
pJrdWR9bWbHjNkUHZ4WxYnYAIiR-krsnV2Ybv2Gb7CATcNfLysMK7w-6y1P1V1Ld-n0oH3YZEiJ=
ev8lmtc6bTcFeXgM82oOQQM6UfUNwl3Jei8sOhQdSaYdrU61NYnS985PwE2rsjTeM8stdAP2VhQ=
nMnbMIs8_h4S9-liplgj_g9tFgbM1NA/4vg/6CNRBhahSx6A6T0zEmMAgw/h28/h001.axjahKv=
ueJq4a3cCUyJu7wLoJzBxZFxbw4IKFPxUA4s" style=3D"color: #0077B6;">who have fo=
rm</a> in coughing up two-goal leads in the competition. However, when your=
 once mighty side has spent the opening months of the season looking more l=
ike a broken washing machine full of spanners falling down an escalator tha=
n an elite football team, complaining about the artistic merit of a highly =
unlikely comeback win seems unnecessarily churlish, even if Matheus Cunha=
=E2=80=99s celebration of the winner was a little over the top.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">United were well represented on Amazon Pri=
me=E2=80=99s coverage, with club alumni Nicky Butt, Ashley Young and Wayne =
Rooney all present to offer their views which reflected a performance of tw=
o incredibly contrasting halves. During the break the trio understandably g=
ot the collective funk on over their former side=E2=80=99s timidity, coward=
ice, and lack of ideas, confidence and belief but less than an hour later t=
hey were whistling a far happier tune.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">=E2=80=9CThe second half was amazing, they=
 were front-footed and they deserved to win the game in the end,=E2=80=9D c=
heered Rooney in his post-match analysis. =E2=80=9CThe first half was terri=
ble, but we saw the passion and fight we wanted in the second half. The cro=
wd got right behind the team and you felt like they could go on to win the =
game.=E2=80=9D While most in the Old Trafford crowd gave the team their ful=
l support, the cameras did inevitably cut to ashen-faced club blazers Jason=
 Wilcox and Omar Berrada half-heartedly applauding their team with all the =
enthusiasm of dinner party hosts forcing a smile as the cat proudly drops a=
 dead mouse on the cheeseboard.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">With United now on an unlikely one-match w=
inning streak, much of the post-match discourse focused on what this win mi=
ght mean for Michael Carrick=E2=80=99s future as head coach. Widely regarde=
d as a dead man walking before the game, he may well have bought himself a =
stay of execution and a scenic detour to the gallows. A pardon seems unlike=
ly but another home win against Aston Villa on Sunday would further endear =
him to fans who really want him to succeed. It would also make life extreme=
ly difficult for his increasingly unpopular employers before an internation=
al break they had almost certainly boxed off as the ideal time to hand him =
his P45.</p>
